# ============================================================
# Production environment -- paste into Dokploy's Environment tab
#
# NOT a file you deploy. Dokploy writes its Environment tab out as the
# .env beside the compose file; this is the list of what to put there,
# and it is the only place these values should live. Nothing here belongs
# in git with real values in it.
#
#   docker compose -f docker-compose.yml -f docker-compose.prod.yml up -d
#
# Full walkthrough: docs/deployment.md
#
# WHAT IS DELIBERATELY ABSENT
#
#   SUPABASE_URL is hardcoded to http://kong:8000 in
#   docker-compose.prod.yml and must not be set here. Inside
#   dokploy-network that is always the address. Set to the public
#   https://supabase.<domain> instead -- which is what happens when the
#   variable is offered -- and internal traffic leaves through Traefik and
#   comes back, slower, and broken whenever TLS or public DNS is.
#
#   NODE_ENV is hardcoded to production for the same reason: development
#   routes pino through a pino-pretty worker that cannot resolve out of a
#   bundled single file, and the process dies at boot with DataCloneError.
#
#   PORT, timeouts, rate limits and the rest have defaults in
#   apps/api/src/env.ts. Add one to the compose file only when you have a
#   reason; that file is the contract of what each service reads.
# ============================================================


# ------------------------------------------------------------
# WHICH ENVIRONMENT THIS IS. Required once you run more than one.
# ------------------------------------------------------------

# Names the compose project, and container names are built from it. Two
# environments both called `ecom` collide on `ecom-api-1`. It is also the
# per-environment DNS alias this API publishes -- `ecom-qa-api` -- which
# is how one shared monitoring stack scrapes the right one.
#   ecom-qa | ecom-uat | ecom-prod
COMPOSE_PROJECT_NAME=ecom-prod

# THIS environment's Supabase network -- the compose project name Dokploy
# gave that stack, plus `_default`. Getting it wrong is the failure worth
# understanding: `kong` is a network ALIAS and aliases are not unique, so
# every Supabase stack claims `kong` on the shared dokploy-network and
# Docker answers round-robin. Naming the specific project's network is
# what makes "which database" a decision rather than a coin flip.
#   supabase-qa_default | supabase-prod_default
SUPABASE_NETWORK=supabase-prod_default


# ------------------------------------------------------------
# REQUIRED. Deployment fails at `compose up` without these, naming
# the variable -- which is a better place to find out than a crash loop.
# ------------------------------------------------------------

# From the Supabase project: Settings -> API. The anon key is public by
# design (it is in the storefront bundle); the service role key bypasses
# RLS entirely and must never reach a browser.
SUPABASE_ANON_KEY=
SUPABASE_SERVICE_ROLE_KEY=

# The SAME secret the Supabase stack runs GoTrue and PostgREST with. The
# API verifies JWTs itself against it rather than calling out per request,
# so a mismatch is not a warning -- every authenticated request 401s while
# the health checks stay green.
SUPABASE_JWT_SECRET=

# Turns on GET /metrics, and Prometheus scrapes with it. ONE value used
# twice: unset it and /metrics 404s; mismatch it and the scrape target
# sits `down` with a 404 that reads like the endpoint is switched off.
#   openssl rand -hex 32
METRICS_TOKEN=

# Grafana's admin password. READ ONLY WHEN THE VOLUME IS FIRST CREATED --
# after that it lives in grafana-data and editing this changes nothing.
# Set it before the first deploy, or you are changing it in the UI later.
# It is also the only authentication anywhere in the monitoring stack.
GRAFANA_PASSWORD=


# ------------------------------------------------------------
# OPTIONAL -- each has a default in apps/api/src/env.ts. Add one when you
# turn that feature on, not before.
# ------------------------------------------------------------

# The admin console's own public URL. Next uses it for metadataBase, so
# canonical and OG links resolve absolutely rather than to localhost.
# Required: there is no sensible default for someone else's domain.
#   https://admin.example.com
ADMIN_URL=

# Storefront and admin origins, comma separated. CORS is closed by
# default, so a browser app calling the API needs its origin here. Server
# to server does not.
#   https://shop.example.com,https://admin.example.com
CORS_ORIGINS=

# --- Payments -----------------------------------------------
# Without these the payment routes refuse rather than pretending. The
# webhook secret is what proves a callback came from Razorpay; captures
# are recorded before they are acted on.
RAZORPAY_KEY_ID=
RAZORPAY_KEY_SECRET=
RAZORPAY_WEBHOOK_SECRET=

# --- Email --------------------------------------------------
# Unset means the outbox fills and drains nowhere, which the ops alerts
# will tell you about. MAIL_PROVIDER picks resend or smtp; supply the one
# set that matches, not both.
MAIL_PROVIDER=
MAIL_FROM=
RESEND_API_KEY=
SMTP_HOST=
SMTP_PORT=587
SMTP_USER=
SMTP_PASS=

# --- Images -------------------------------------------------
# STORAGE_PUBLIC_URL should be the CDN or R2 custom domain, never the
# /storage/v1/object/public/... path -- that proxies every byte through
# your storage container, which is the one reason to be on R2 thrown
# away. Unset means the image routes answer 503 rather than pretending.
STORAGE_BUCKET=
STORAGE_PUBLIC_URL=

# --- Jobs ---------------------------------------------------
# Shared secret for the external job trigger endpoint. The in-process
# tick runs without it; this only gates calling the endpoint by hand.
JOBS_SECRET=

# --- Docs ---------------------------------------------------
# Swagger UI at /docs and the OpenAPI document. Defaults to false in
# production: it publishes your whole route table to anyone who asks.
DOCS_PUBLIC=false

# --- Monitoring ---------------------------------------------
# Grafana's public URL, if you give it a domain. Without it, links Grafana
# generates -- alert notifications especially -- point at localhost.
#   https://grafana.example.com
GRAFANA_ROOT_URL=

# The external network shared with Supabase. The default is correct on
# Dokploy; set it only if your network is named something else.
# MONITORING_API_NETWORK=dokploy-network
